      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-751",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{(-1)^{0}{x}^{2\\cdot 0}}{(2\\cdot 0)!} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{1}{x}^{2\\cdot 1}}{(2\\cdot 1)!} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}{x}^{2\\cdot 2}}{(2\\cdot 2)!} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{(-1)^{3}{x}^{2\\cdot 3}}{(2\\cdot 3)!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{(-1)^n x^{2n}}{(2n)!} \\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
